1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,533
Dad, what's this?

2
00:00:01,533 --> 00:00:03,133
Huh? This… is mine!

3
00:00:04,767 --> 00:00:07,833
In grade school, I saved my homework on these.

4
00:00:07,833 --> 00:00:09,500
Swapped games with friends, too.

5
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:11,333
Sometimes it all vanished and I cried…

6
00:00:11,333 --> 00:00:14,233
The floppy disk. It stored computer data.

7
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:17,700
Back then this was the biggest storage in the world!

8
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:22,700
Wrong answer, one. Even then, the hard disk inside the computer held far more.

9
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:27,233
1.44 megabytes per disk. Barely enough for one photo today.

10
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:28,567
What's inside?

11
00:00:28,567 --> 00:00:30,200
A hard plastic shell,

12
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:31,667
a metal shutter that keeps out dust,

13
00:00:31,667 --> 00:00:33,167
soft cloth that wipes the disk,

14
00:00:33,167 --> 00:00:36,300
and a thin film disk coated with magnetic powder.

15
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:37,767
The drive spins the disk,

16
00:00:37,767 --> 00:00:41,367
and a tiny magnetic head marks each spot N or S.

17
00:00:41,367 --> 00:00:44,833
Those N's and S's are the computer's 1's and 0's.

18
00:00:44,833 --> 00:00:47,333
It's hard, so why is it called floppy?

19
00:00:47,333 --> 00:00:52,267
The first big ones, from 1971, really were floppy.

20
00:00:52,267 --> 00:00:53,733
So why don't we use them now?

21
00:00:53,733 --> 00:00:55,833
Bigger ones kept coming.

22
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:58,333
One shiny CD held about 500 floppies.

23
00:00:58,333 --> 00:01:00,933
A finger-size USB stick held even more.

24
00:01:00,933 --> 00:01:04,333
And now, the cloud! We put it up on the clouds!

25
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:05,633
Wrong answer, two.

26
00:01:05,633 --> 00:01:09,733
The cloud means storing it in huge computer warehouses across the internet.

27
00:01:09,733 --> 00:01:12,900
One phone today holds more than 80,000 floppies.

28
00:01:12,900 --> 00:01:14,600
Wait, this picture is right here!

29
00:01:14,600 --> 00:01:17,933
The disk is gone, but it lives on as the save icon.

30
00:01:17,933 --> 00:01:20,167
So is your homework still in here?

31
00:01:20,167 --> 00:01:21,900
…There's no machine left to open it.

32
00:01:22,067 --> 00:01:23,933
I'm… Modapman.
